# React Native / Expo — CursorRules Pro

You are an expert React Native and Expo developer building production mobile apps. Follow these rules strictly.

## Project Conventions
- Use Expo for managed workflow unless specific native modules require bare React Native.
- File naming: kebab-case for components, PascalCase for screen components.
- Structure: `app/` for screens (Expo Router), `components/` for reusable UI, `hooks/` for custom hooks.
- Use absolute imports with path aliases: `@/components`, `@/hooks`, `@/utils`.
- TypeScript strict mode — type everything, no `any`.

## TypeScript
- Type all component props with interfaces: `interface ButtonProps { ... }`.
- Use `React.FC<Props>` or explicit return types for components.
- Type navigation with stack/tab param lists: `export type RootStackParamList = { ... }`.
- Use `as const` for constant objects and arrays.
- Type custom hooks with explicit return types.

## Navigation (Expo Router)
- Use file-based routing in `app/` directory: `app/index.tsx`, `app/profile.tsx`.
- Dynamic routes: `app/posts/[id].tsx` with `useLocalSearchParams()`.
- Layouts: `_layout.tsx` for nested navigators (tabs, stacks).
- Use `<Link href="...">` for navigation, `router.push()` for programmatic navigation.
- Protect routes with auth guards in layout files.

## Components & UI
- Use functional components exclusively — no class components.
- Separate presentational and container components.
- Use StyleSheet.create() for styles, not inline styles (performance).
- Platform-specific code: `Platform.OS === 'ios'` or `.ios.tsx` / `.android.tsx` files.
- Use FlatList/SectionList for lists, never ScrollView with .map() for large data.
- Implement proper list optimization: `keyExtractor`, `getItemLayout`, `windowSize`.

## State Management
- Local state: `useState` and `useReducer` for component-level state.
- Global state: Zustand (preferred) or Redux Toolkit.
- Server state: TanStack Query (React Query) for API data.
- Avoid prop drilling — use context or state management library.
- Keep state as close to where it's used as possible.

## Data Fetching
- Use TanStack Query for API calls with automatic caching and refetching.
- Define query keys as constants: `const QUERY_KEYS = { posts: 'posts', user: (id) => ['user', id] }`.
- Use mutations for API writes with optimistic updates.
- Handle loading and error states in every data-fetching component.
- Implement pull-to-refresh with RefreshControl on FlatLists.

## Performance
- Memoize expensive computations with `useMemo`.
- Memoize callbacks passed to child components with `useCallback`.
- Use `React.memo()` for components that re-render often with same props.
- Optimize FlatList: implement `getItemLayout` for fixed-height items, use `removeClippedSubviews`.
- Lazy load screens and heavy components with `React.lazy()`.
- Profile with React Native Performance Monitor (Flipper) and identify bottlenecks.

## Styling
- Use StyleSheet.create() for styles — better performance than inline.
- Organize styles at bottom of file or separate `styles.ts` file.
- Use design tokens for colors, spacing, typography: `theme.colors.primary`, `theme.spacing.md`.
- Responsive design: Use Dimensions API or responsive libraries (react-native-responsive-screen).
- Dark mode support: Use `useColorScheme()` hook and conditional styles.

## Platform-Specific Code
- Use `Platform.select()` for small differences: `Platform.select({ ios: 10, android: 12 })`.
- Use separate files for major differences: `Button.ios.tsx`, `Button.android.tsx`.
- Test on both iOS and Android regularly — don't assume parity.
- Handle safe area insets: Use `react-native-safe-area-context` for notches/home indicators.
- Respect platform UI conventions (navigation patterns, gestures, animations).

## Forms & Validation
- Use `react-hook-form` for form state management.
- Validate with Zod schemas integrated with react-hook-form.
- Use controlled inputs for single-source-of-truth state.
- Handle keyboard dismissal: `Keyboard.dismiss()` or `TouchableWithoutFeedback`.
- Use `KeyboardAvoidingView` for forms (proper behavior prop for iOS/Android).

## Authentication
- Store tokens securely with `expo-secure-store` (encrypted storage).
- Never store sensitive data in AsyncStorage (unencrypted).
- Implement auto-login on app launch (check token, refresh if needed).
- Protect routes with auth guard in `_layout.tsx`.
- Clear all auth data on logout (token, user state).

## Networking
- Use fetch or axios for API calls (prefer axios for interceptors).
- Set base URL in config: `axios.defaults.baseURL = API_URL`.
- Add auth token to all requests with axios interceptor.
- Handle network errors gracefully (offline mode, retry logic).
- Use NetInfo to detect online/offline status.

## Assets & Media
- Use Expo Image for optimized image loading with caching.
- Store images in `assets/images/`, use `require()` for static images.
- Use vector icons with `@expo/vector-icons` (FontAwesome, Ionicons, etc.).
- Optimize image sizes for mobile (use appropriate resolutions, compress).
- Use Expo Asset for preloading critical assets on app launch.

## Native Modules & APIs
- Use Expo SDK modules when available (Camera, Location, Notifications).
- For custom native code, eject to bare workflow or use Expo Development Builds.
- Request permissions before accessing native APIs: `requestPermissionsAsync()`.
- Handle permission denial gracefully (show explanation, link to settings).
- Test native features on physical devices, not just simulators.

## Notifications
- Use Expo Notifications for push notifications.
- Request permission on iOS, auto-granted on Android.
- Handle foreground, background, and notification-tap interactions.
- Store push token in backend for sending notifications.
- Test notification scenarios: app foreground, background, killed.

## Error Handling
- Wrap app in error boundary to catch React errors.
- Log errors to crash reporting service (Sentry, Bugsnag).
- Show user-friendly error messages, log technical details.
- Handle async errors in try-catch blocks.
- Provide fallback UI for failed components.

## Testing
- Write unit tests for utils, hooks, and business logic with Jest.
- Write component tests with React Native Testing Library.
- Test user interactions: button presses, form inputs, navigation.
- Mock API calls and native modules in tests.
- Test on both platforms (iOS simulator + Android emulator).

## Build & Deployment
- Use EAS Build for cloud-based builds (iOS + Android).
- Use EAS Update for over-the-air updates (fixes, content changes).
- Increment version numbers in app.json for each release.
- Test on TestFlight (iOS) and Google Play Internal Testing before production.
- Set up separate builds for dev, staging, production environments.

## Common Patterns
- Screen wrapper: Create `<ScreenWrapper>` with safe area and keyboard handling.
- Loading indicator: Show centered spinner during data loading.
- Empty states: Show helpful message when lists are empty.
- Pull-to-refresh: Implement on all data-fetching lists.
- Error retry: Provide "Try Again" button on API errors.

## Common Mistakes to Avoid
- Do NOT use ScrollView with .map() for long lists — use FlatList.
- Do NOT use inline styles for static styles — use StyleSheet.create().
- Do NOT forget to handle loading and error states in data-fetching components.
- Do NOT store sensitive data in AsyncStorage — use SecureStore.
- Do NOT forget to request permissions before using native APIs.
- Do NOT use class components — use functional components with hooks.
- Do NOT mutate state directly — use setState or state management library methods.
- Do NOT forget platform-specific behavior (keyboard, safe areas, navigation).
